{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-discipline", "file": "references/session-0ee559ac-review.md", "content": "# Session review: evidence extraction versus demo chaining

In this instance, four paid examples were delivered, but multiple requests were made without extracting the intervening input/output deltas. A speculative test-grid submission then failed. The examples varied substantially in geometry and color; their observable changes were sparse and did not justify a clear test edit from visual similarity alone. Repeated skill/reference browsing also displaced actual analysis. No transformation was established here.

Reusable procedure: after every returned pair, record dimensions, exact changed coordinates/runs, and conspicuous unchanged candidates; separate the local edit operation from the rule selecting its target. Update candidate test projections before any further action. Only request another example when surviving rules disagree on the test and a plausible demo feature would distinguish them. After incorrect feedback, reject the complete hypothesis and rebuild; do not patch the guessed output. Once relevant guidance is loaded, return to coordinate-level evidence rather than browsing overlapping notes. Honor exact one-action JSON contracts throughout.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review-discipline/references/session-0ee559ac-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous object-cleaning demos: session note

When several differently sized grids contain solid monochrome rectangles among sparse alien-colored marks, don't infer a simple global recoloring from palette overlap. Compare each pair's deltas and classify objects: the demos can show preservation of intact rectangles while isolated noise is erased and intrusions inside a target object are repaired. Record separately (a) isolated marks cleared to the background, (b) interior foreign-color cells replaced by the enclosing object's color, (c) genuine missing cells filled to complete a rectangle, and (d) non-target/background regions left unchanged. Do not automatically fill every connected component's bounding box: validate each proposed target's original geometry and its output mask. The common operation may be shape- or context-selected rather than applying to every colored cell.

After a wrong answer, reject the entire selection/repair hypothesis and replay all demos; changing only the set of speckles or rectangle bounds is not sufficient. A new paid example is worthwhile only when surviving selection rules predict different test outputs. Mechanically generate and validate the output grid before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-discipline", "file": "references/serialized-grid-validation.md", "content": "# Serialized output validation for ARC stream grids

Large generated grids are vulnerable to a one-cell omission when transcribed into inline JSON, even when the transformation itself is plausible. Build the output as a structured list-of-lists, then assert: (1) row count equals expected height, (2) every row has exactly expected width, (3) all cell values are integers in 0–9. Serialize that exact structure to JSON and use the serialized result without hand-editing. If feedback says “not a valid ARC grid,” audit shape and serialization first; that message does not establish whether the transformation is semantically correct.

This check validates only format, not the ARC rule. Independently replay the inferred operation against demos and verify changed and unchanged cells before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review-discipline/references/serialized-grid-validation.md"}
